diff --git a/public/framework/audio/Recite/doc/architecture.md b/public/framework/audio/Recite/doc/architecture.md
index 3972549fe..506f0eac4 100644
--- a/public/framework/audio/Recite/doc/architecture.md
+++ b/public/framework/audio/Recite/doc/architecture.md
@@ -35,9 +35,15 @@ Built 2026-11-01 in the voice-recite task ([requirements](/framework/ai/sessions
 - A console race in `core/Test`'s `TestBlocks.js` (concurrent `test()` calls sharing one global captor) used to throw `Recite: a listener failed TypeError: fn is not a function` from `Recite.changed()` during a `play()` call — caught, never failed a test, but failed smoke. `changed()` now skips a non-function listener instead of calling and catching it, which is strictly safer regardless of cause; `on()`/`set()` are unchanged.
 - **No flood, piece 1: the quiet window is 2.5 s, not unified into one field.** `Recite.turn_wait_ms` and `BargeIn.quiet_ms` both moved from 1500 to 2500 (within the owner's 2–3 s ask), but stay two separate fields rather than one shared setting: several existing tests set `r.barge.quiet_ms` directly to speed themselves up, and the two timers gate different moments (starting a fresh reply that would talk over the owner, vs. resuming one already cut off by a barge-in) — unifying them is a real simplification, still open, not done in this merge.
 - **No flood, piece 2: an agent update is never read in full — it collapses to one line, and the direct answer plays first.** `from_wire()` used to drop any assistant reply whose `from.id` wasn't `"smart"` entirely; now `Recite.agent_update(text, id, from_id)` reads it instead — but only when `from_id` matches `Recite.AGENT_ID` (`mastermind-`/`minion-`/`reviewer-`/`clarity-`), not for every non-"smart" id. `"fast"` (the fast seat), `"revise"` (every voice revision of the owner's own sentence) and `"card"` (card conversations) post an assistant chat line too, but are not agent updates — they still fall through to the old drop, exactly as before this task (review finding, read-aloud-b 2026-11-02: `revise` fires on every dictated sentence, so collapsing it to a spoken line would have added the very flood this task was meant to stop). Collapsed, as `"I have an update on {label}, it is in the chat."` (`Recite.agent_label()`: the `mastermind-`/`minion-`/`reviewer-`/`clarity-` prefix off, dashes and underscores to spaces — a rough heuristic, not a lookup of the agent's own title). While `cue_pending` is true (the owner's own line landed and the direct "smart" answer hasn't arrived yet), the update waits in `pending_updates` instead of queuing, so it can never play ahead of — or instead of — the answer the owner is actually waiting for; `flush_updates()` releases it, behind whatever just queued, the moment that answer's own `final()`/`partial()` call returns (both `from_wire()` call sites call it right after), or after `update_hold_ms` (4 s) regardless, so a held update is never lost if the owner's line turns out not to need a direct reply at all. With no answer pending, an update just queues immediately like any other reply — there is nothing to hold it behind. `stop()` clears `pending_updates` and the hold timer along with the rest of the queue.
